'''Gnau''' is a language spoken in [[Papua New Guinea]].  It is part of the [[Torricelli languages|Torricelli]] language family. "Gnau" is the word for "no" in the language, and was named this via naming by convention.<ref>{{cite journal | url=https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/j.1834-4461.1974.tb01836.x | doi=10.1002/j.1834-4461.1974.tb01836.x | title=Gnau1 Anatomy and Vocabulary for Illnesses | date=1974 | last1=Lewis | first1=Gilbert | journal=Oceania | volume=45 | pages=50–78 | url-access=subscription }}</ref> The Gnau people do not identify themselves as a unified group across multiple settlements, despite their shared language.<ref>{{cite web |title=Gnau |url=https://www.encyclopedia.com/humanities/encyclopedias-almanacs-transcripts-and-maps/gnau |website=Encyclopedia.com |access-date=17 September 2023}}</ref>

